ey still are have core receptors in  the in the sense  in the nature of these kinases which  have diversified  so uh essentially these  nlrs are pretty much always forming  these  complex networks and we think that this  network  feature of the immune system of the  receptors  is a very important feature uh which  allows them to diversify and allows them  also to be  to have a more robust immune response so  for instance  the sensors are now free to acquire new  domain  acquire new specificity diversion the  sequence without  being constrained by the capacity to  maintain the  uh ability to trigger the cell death so  for example they can acquire and  terminal integrations  whereas if they were helpers they  wouldn't be able to do that so having uh  essentially a network of receptors  uh makes the system a lot more evolvable  and probably a lot more robust  so i'll end there and  i want to thank the folks so i talked  about  jihan and aki uh but all the folks here  in particular more recently we have lida  and abbas  mauricio and toshi all of these really  contributed quite a lot to the nrc  project and more recently to the zarwan  evolution project  colleagues and collaborators um toga  boscort at imperial college mark  benfield that john lennis  and rio terauchi at  kyoto university and uh all the members  of my lab and also my colleagues at the  same relapse so thank you  uh so much and i hope this time for  questions  hi thanks so much sophia for the  excellent seminar um so  we have a few minutes for questions  and we can try to do hand raising or  you can type in the chat um and  i see that's a applause okay  so we have a question um first from jeff  doyle he asked does the phylogeny  correctly depict evolutionary  relationships in the nrcr gene family  our genes are known to be recomogenic so  perhaps a network approach would be more  suitable  yes so that's really interesting  uh point and in fact one of the  one of the projects we have now is  actually trying to predict  connections between nlrs so one of the  projects we have  is mining plant genomes for nlrs  and looking for clusters  somebody has a their phone but uh anyway  so looking for clusters of nlrs that  carry  both mother and non-mother uh nlrs  in that same cluster and and then having  the high poisons that  the mothers are forming the helpers and  the non-mother the  senses so this is um this is something  indeed we are really interested in  this has also implications for plant  breeding um  because um in some cases if the helper  happens to be diverse in your germplasm  you need to move two genes not just a  single gene to  to introduce resistance and  after we discovered the network we  actually established couple of  collaborations with industry uh just to  do  that because it's well known in plant  breeding that very often you have  resistance in one germplasm but if you  move it to a different  background um in an elite variety for  example  uh the resistance is either uh weaker  or sometimes even it's lost  so yeah that's a really good good  question  great and then we have a question from  adam bogdanov  um he says that it appears the sensor  helper divergence happened more than  once  um any sensor helper pairs that aren't  from the same ancient pair  um convergently involved dependencies  also please tell us about the gfp  nrc for localization in your slide  yeah so um okay so  uh the first question is that yes there  are  there are definitely pairs that are  stuck in that configuration  over evolutionary time so in rice for  example there are examples of that  the pia rga4 iga5 is an example  the one i mentioned in our system is an  rc0 so somehow the nrc0  is linked so that's an nrc that's  nnrc that is in the phylogeny appears to  be ancestral it's linked to nrc 0  dependent sensors  and that's conserved anywhere from  cotton coffee to tomato for example  so that's really um not cotton sorry  carrot carrot coffee and tomato  and and so that's quite quite surprising  case  so i'm not so sure about what the reason  is it could be that  they have specialized and are now  expressed by for example in specific  tissue and are  maybe dedicated to given pathogen or it  could be that the way they  mechanistically function  they cannot be separated so for example  if you have a negative regulator  then you really become a single genetic  unit and you can't separate that  otherwise there would be  a cost for the plant so i think that's a  really interesting question i think um  that's something we're very much aware  of and we're really looking into that  and trying to uh dig out cases where  um you have uh pairs that are  retained as pairs and and when they  duplicate they duplicate  as pairs actually so uh there are cases  like that  uh the second question is the gfp and  rc4 localization is  partly to the membrane uh there's just  too much gfp around so it's  um it's kind of hard to really uh say  but  it does the localization does overlap  with the plasma membrane  i should however mention that my  colleague toga boscort at imperial  college  does send biology of the nrcs  and they localize an rc4 to the  hostorial interface  so we do think that the nrc4 actually is  not functioning at the plasma membrane  but function at the level of the  extra-historian membrane  some of you may have heard talks by toga  or his students  on that topic they haven't yet published  the work but um  so we don't think that it's necessarily  the plasma membrane that's a target  could be other endomembrane compartments  great and then we have another question  uh from sharon tsuwanki  if the nlr system is converts to nrcs  and ultimately to pcd master regulators  then why pathogens are not directly  targeting the nrch and downstream  pathways  wow that's the question that's the slide  i removed  we have a project that we started very  early on uh we hypothesized early on  that  um there would be effectors that  suppress the nrc network and will target  the helpers  essentially it's like back one right for  the prr so if you're back one then  uh you're gonna be magnet for pathogen  effectors  so we hypothesize that the nrc helpers  are going to be  targets for solar nature's pathogens  and we screened actually um effectors  for that we have two candidates that we  followed up one is  from uh cyst nematode potato cyst  nematode  and the other ones from phytophthora  infestance interestingly enough  they are act at different levels the  systematic defector binds directly to um  to the nb domain of the nrcs  uh the phytophtha one uh acts through a  target  so it's there's that's indirect  suppression  but that's a great question indeed  that's a prediction you would make when  you have networks like that  and that could explain also why there  are multiple nrcs that have evolved  perhaps they  duplicated to evade pathogen suppression  that's one of the things that makes the  system more robust so if you have  multiple nrcs  working redundantly then it's harder for  pathogen to suppress all of them  great so if there are any more questions  please put them in the chat  um and i'd like to remind the graduate  students and postdocs  that after the starting around 1 50 i'll  open the alternate zoom room  and we'll have our lunch discussion  so i don't see any more questions  yet but if you have any more  you can send them to sophie and sophie  and you can take a break for a few  minutes and then join us in the  other zoom room when you're ready okay i  will and if anyone has any questions  please  get in touch directly or through twitter  or um  any other means happy to answer any  questions great  thank you so much thank you thank you  again and thank you everyone thanks alan  thanks  zoe thank you  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu


